WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.723
I shot a gun at nine years old.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.860
In the meet of the loud guns,
you cannot hear your gun

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.833
and that sometime can be terrifying.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.720
So you come to your conscience
by seeing a gun shaking,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.870
that's when you know
it's actually working.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.320
Emmanuel Jal was a child soldier

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.743
during the brutal Sudanese
civil war in the 1990's.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.110
Where government forces in
the predominantly Muslim north

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.460
fought rebels in the south.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.260
Taken from his home, he was beaten

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.820
brainwashed and trained
by the rebels to fight.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.130
After four years of fighting, he managed

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.223
an epic escape to create a new life.

00:00:45.888 --> 00:00:48.609
♪ I believe I've survived for a reason, ♪

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:53.609
♪ To tell my story, to touch lives. ♪

00:00:55.904 --> 00:00:58.595
♪ I believe I've survived for a reason, ♪

00:00:58.595 --> 00:01:03.190
♪ To tell my story, to touch lives. ♪

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.740
Mr. Jal was sent to train to be a soldier

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.660
at the age of eight by his father

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.210
who was the rebels' chief of police.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.513
The training was brutal.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.180
We were welcomed with a beating.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.930
The violence that was given to us,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.363
I can't even describe it.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:26.390
I look at as a psychological
way to break into us

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.383
so they can mold us to what they want.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.657
He survived the training

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:34.610
and was given a gun, ready for revenge.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.690
That war took the soul of my village,

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.440
and my aunties died during that war.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.610
All my uncles died during that war.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.100
My mother was claimed by that war.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.540
As a kid, I wanted two things.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.800
To get a bike, I wanted to get a bicycle.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.050
And also to kill as many
Arabs and Muslims as possible.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.300
Mr. Jal and the other boys,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.930
had been groomed to feel
a sense of camaraderie,

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.680
to fight for each other.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.170
At first, the violence was exciting.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.170
The beat of the AK-47
itself is so empowering.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.503
Violence is fun.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:15.270
There's an excitement when they give you

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.743
a gun into your hand, you're excited.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.910
Memories from childhood can be hazy.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.870
And some have disputed Mr. Jal's claims,

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.390
but he says after four years of fighting

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.293
he escaped the rebel commanders.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.990
In the dead of night he, and
400 other child soldiers, fled.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.560
If they were recaptured,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.070
they would've almost
certainly been killed.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.720
The boys trekked for three months

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.990
across the country facing starvation.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:48.050
I call this the lowest
point I have ever been,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.800
because we drunk our own urine.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.133
Other soldiers started eating dead bodies.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.570
One of the escapees managed

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.663
to reach a village and raise the alarm.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:06.033
Mr. Jal says only 16 of
the 400 boys survived.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.040
We got rescued and I
met a British aid worker

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.050
called Emma McCune.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.130
And she's the one that smuggled me

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.420
through a plane into Kenya.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.830
That became the turning point of my life,

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.493
where I was able to go to school.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.950
Mrs. McCune was married
to Mr. Jal's uncle,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.200
the rebel leader Riek Machar.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.663
She died in a car crash
shortly afterwards.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.750
So Mr. Jal had to make his own way

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.823
as a teenager in the Kenyan slums.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:39.883
But something saved him.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.130
As a way of dealing with the
trauma he had experienced,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.833
he started rapping and discovered hip-hop.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:55.420
It's got into music because
I found music was the place

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.463
I was able to become a child again.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.000
In 2004, his debut album
was a hit in Kenya.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.670
The single appeared on
a U.K. charity album

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.033
and he played at Nelson
Mandela's 90th birthday concert.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.400
In the last decade, Mr.
Jal has traveled the world

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.543
performing and sharing his
experiences through his music,

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.890
with the likes of Alicia
Keys and Nelly Furtado.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:35.573
We want peace, big love to Emmanuel Jal.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.860
He uses his music to be a

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.083
political activist and peace advocate.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.440
The challenges that I've been
through, I accepted them.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.930
I accepted my childhood experience.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.560
Every person who has become successful,

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.720
has gone through fire.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.280
Mr. Jal is one of the lucky ones.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.330
According to the U.N., tens
of thousands of children

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.100
have been recruited as child soldiers

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.683
across 20 countries this year alone.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.570
Children's place should be school.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:13.606
Children should be taught to love.

00:05:13.606 --> 00:05:16.650
They should be taught to
explore their imagination,

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.630
not to be trained to kill people,

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.713
not to be exposed to violence.

